    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1137</span><span class="rf-text">Saladin is born in Tikrit, by his biographer's account on the night his family left the town</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1169</span><span class="rf-text">The Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adid">al-Adid</a> appoints him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> after the death of his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1171</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adid">Al-Adid</a> dies and the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> is proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1174</span><span class="rf-text">After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>'s death he rides to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> with 700 horsemen and enters it amid general acclamation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1175</span><span class="rf-text">He defeats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengids</a> at the Horns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> and is declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1187</span><span class="rf-text">He largely annihilates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> capitulates to him</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1192</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1192">Treaty of Jaffa</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> ends the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1193</span><span class="rf-text">He dies of a fever at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi.jpg" alt="Imad al-Din Zengi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruler_in_Turkic_dress_(long_braids,_fur_hat,_boots,_fitting_coat),_in_the_Maqamat_of_al-Hariri,_1237_CE,_probably_Baghdad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, 1237 CE, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saladin was born in Tikrit in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. His personal name was Yusuf; Salah ad-Din is an honorific meaning "Righteousness of the Faith". His parents were of Kurdish Rawadiya ancestry and had come from the village of Ajdanakan near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvin">Dvin</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. His father, Najm al-Din Ayyub, was warden of the fortress of Tikrit, where in 1132 he gave refuge to the defeated army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a>, Atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. In 1137 the family was banished from Tikrit after Ayyub's brother Asad al-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> killed a friend of the governor; according to Baha al-Din ibn Shaddad, Saladin's contemporary biographer, he was born the night they left.</p><p>In 1139 the family moved to Mosul, and Zengi made Ayyub commander of his fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a>. Saladin later lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>; Zengi's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> became leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengids</a>. Information on his childhood is scarce. Several sources claim that he was more interested in religious studies than in joining the military; he knew the genealogies and histories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and the Hamasah of Abu Tammam by heart, spoke Kurdish and Arabic, and knew Turkish and Persian.</p><p>His military career began under his uncle Shirkuh, a commander of Nur al-Din. In 1164, aged 26, he accompanied Shirkuh's expedition to restore Shawar as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adid">al-Adid</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. At the Battle of al-Babein, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, he commanded the right wing of the Zengid army, though Muslim sources of the time place him with the baggage of the centre. In 1167 he was left to hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and withstood a siege by a larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a>–Egyptian force for three months.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Amalric I of Jerusalem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric I of Jerusalem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amalric_receives_a_message_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1168 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric I of Jerusalem</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and Shawar again asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> for help. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> and Saladin arrived in 1169, Amalric withdrew, and Shirkuh had Shawar executed and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, but died later that year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adid">Al-Adid</a> appointed Saladin in his place. The accounts differ on why: Ibn al-Athir claims the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> was told that "there is no one weaker or younger" than Saladin, while Imad al-Din al-Isfahani wrote that the Zengid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> forced the caliph to "invest him as vizier". He was inaugurated on 26 March.</p><p>Later that year he learned of a plot against him and had its chief conspirator, the controller of the Fatimid palace, arrested and killed. The next day 50,000 Black African soldiers of the Fatimid army revolted; by 23 August he had quelled the uprising. Towards the end of 1169 he defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a>–Byzantine force near Damietta. He gave members of his family high positions and founded colleges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> law. In 1171, urged by Nur al-Din, he restored Abbasid suzerainty: al-Adid died on 13 September, and five days later the Abbasid caliph was named in the Friday sermon in Cairo.</p><p>His brother Turan-Shah drove off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> and in 1174 was sent to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. In the early summer of 1174 Nur al-Din was mustering an army, apparently against Egypt, but he died on 15 May, leaving an eleven-year-old son, as-Salih. When the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> asked for help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, Saladin crossed the desert with 700 picked horsemen and entered Damascus on 23 November amid general acclamation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin.jpg" alt="Battle of Hattin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saladin_Guy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matthew Paris, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> Saladin set about taking the cities that had belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a>, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and captured the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> in March 1175. On 13 April 1175 he defeated the combined Zengid forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and Aleppo at the Horns of Hama. He then proclaimed himself king, dropped as-Salih's name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a> and the coinage, and was declared "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>" by the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. A second victory at the Sultan's Mound in 1176 led to a treaty in which Aleppo recognised his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over his conquests.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> he had the city walls repaired and extended and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel">Cairo Citadel</a>. He left Egypt for the last time on 11 May 1182. In northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> he took Edessa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a> and Nusaybin and captured Amid, but failed to take Mosul; Aleppo was handed to him by negotiation on 12 June 1183, and a peace was signed with Mosul in March 1186.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a> attacked a Muslim caravan during a truce, Saladin resolved to topple the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>. On 4 July 1187 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> force was largely annihilated and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a>, king consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, captured. Saladin took almost every Crusader city, and Jerusalem capitulated on 2 October 1187 after a siege. Under the terms, each Frank could buy freedom for a ransom; Saladin let many families who could not pay leave, about 18,000 of the poor were ransomed by a collection, and another 15,000 were left to be enslaved. He permitted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to resettle in the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-iv-of-jerusalem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baldwin-iv-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Baldwin IV of Jerusalem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-iv-of-jerusalem">Baldwin IV of Jerusalem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Baldwin_IV.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guillermo de Tiro, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saladin's first opponents were Muslim. His Zengid rivals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> claimed that he had "forgotten his own condition" as a servant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> and risen "in rebellion against his Lord" by besieging his master's son. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Order of Assassins</a>, at odds with him since he replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, twice tried to kill him, in May 1175 and at the siege of A'zaz in 1176; he laid waste their countryside in August 1176 without taking a fort, and later maintained cooperative relations with their leader Sinan. Among Egyptian Shi'as he is called "Kharab al-Din", "the destroyer of religion", for dismantling the Fatimids.</p><p>He suffered defeats. On 25 November 1177 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-iv-of-jerusalem">Baldwin IV of Jerusalem</a> surprised his army at Montgisard, and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> with a small remnant. He twice besieged Raynald's fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> without success, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a>, withstood two sieges.</p><p>His wars had their cruelties. In 1178 he had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> captives executed for "plundering and laying waste the lands of the Faithful"; in 1183 the 170 survivors of Raynald's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> raid were killed on his orders in various Muslim cities; and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> he was personally responsible for the execution of Raynald. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a> his soldiers broke discipline and plundered the city. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> most of the foot soldiers were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. According to Ibn Shaddad he "hated the philosophers"; in 1191 he ordered his son to execute the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> philosopher Yahya al-Suhrawardi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. In Aleppo he replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> courts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> administration despite a promise not to interfere.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england.jpg" alt="Richard I of England" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_Fontevraud_Abbey_Richard_I_effigy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adam Bishop, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> prompted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> (1189–1192). King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> led the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, took the city and executed almost 3,000 Muslim prisoners of war; Baha al-Din recorded differing explanations of the massacre and concluded, "God alone knows what the real reason was." On 7 September 1191 Saladin's forces suffered heavy losses at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-arsuf">Battle of Arsuf</a> and were forced to withdraw. Richard occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a>, and Saladin dismantled the fortifications of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> to keep the city from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> hands.</p><p>The two rulers exchanged envoys. Richard proposed that his sister Joan marry Saladin's brother, with Jerusalem as their wedding gift, but Saladin rejected the idea when Richard insisted that the brother convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. In January 1192 Richard's army reached Beit Nuba, twelve miles from Jerusalem, and withdrew without attacking. In July Saladin besieged Jaffa and very nearly captured it before Richard arrived and defeated him outside the city. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1192">Treaty of Jaffa</a> Richard agreed to demolish the fortifications of Ascalon and Saladin recognised Crusader control of the coast from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> to Jaffa; Christians could travel to Jerusalem as unarmed pilgrims, and there was to be peace for three years.</p><p>Saladin died of a fever at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> on 4 March 1193, not long after Richard's departure, and was buried in a mausoleum in the garden outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Umayyad Mosque</a>. Imad al-Din writes that he was survived by seventeen sons and one daughter; among them were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a>, emir of Damascus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a>, sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and al-Zahir, emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri.jpg" alt="Dante Alighieri" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bargello_-_Kapelle_Fresko_2a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wolfgang Sauber, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a> that Saladin founded outlived him by only 57 years, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> persisted until the late 13th century, but 1187 marked a turning point in the Christian military effort in the region. In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> he was known as a model of kingship and of regal generosity. As early as 1202/03 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walther-von-der-vogelweide">Walther von der Vogelweide</a> urged the German king to be more like him; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> placed him among the virtuous non-Christians in limbo, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Boccaccio</a> depicted him favourably. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing">Lessing</a>'s play Nathan the Wise (1779) and above all Sir Walter Scott's novel The Talisman (1825) shaped the romantic modern view.</p><p>In the Muslim world his reputation had been largely forgotten, eclipsed by more successful figures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The visit of the German emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> to his tomb in 1898, coupled with anti-imperialist sentiment, encouraged the image of Saladin in the Arab world as a hero of the struggle against the West, building on the one created by Scott and other Europeans. With the rise of Arab nationalism in the 20th century his recapture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> became an inspiration for opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a>, and the Eagle of Saladin became the symbol of revolutionary Egypt and of several other Arab states.</p><p>He has been described as the most famous Kurd in history and is a prominent figure in Arab, Turkish and Kurdish culture. Mark Cartwright writes that appreciation of his diplomacy and leadership in both Muslim and Christian sources of his time suggests that he "is indeed worthy of his position as one of the great medieval leaders".</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>A Kurdish officer in Zengid service, Saladin became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1169, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and united Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> under his rule. He destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1187, then held it against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1192">Treaty of Jaffa</a> of 1192.</em></p>

